Builder Design Pattern — Short Recap
What is Builder Pattern?
The Builder Design Pattern is a creational design pattern used to create complex objects
step by step.

It is useful when a class has many fields/parameters, especially when some are optional.

Why use Builder Pattern?
Makes object creation easier to read.
Avoids constructors with many parameters.
Allows setting only the required fields.
Supports method chaining.
Keeps the final object separate from the object-creation process.
Can help create immutable objects.
What your User class is doing

Your User class represents the actual object that you want to create.

User contains the final data: userId, userName, and emailId.
Its constructor is private, so users cannot directly create a User object.
UserBuilder is responsible for collecting all the required values.
Each setter adds a value and returns the same builder, which enables chaining.
builder() provides a convenient way to create a new builder.
build() takes the collected values from the builder and creates the final User object.
User has only getters, not setters, so once created, its values cannot be changed.
Simple flow to remember

Builder → Collect values → Configure object → Build → Final User object

Key interview point
Builder Pattern separates the construction of an object from the object itself and allows the
object to be created step by step